<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <!--
    All-files access is the whole reason a sideloaded APK can do this job at all. SAFT rewrites
    940 MB archives in place; Android's document framework would make that unusably slow, and it
    cannot reach a game folder the user chose anyway. This permission gives ordinary file access to
    both internal shared storage and a removable SD card, which is where the game usually lives.

    It is also why this app is not a Play Store app: Google restricts this permission to file
    managers and similar. For a modding tool distributed on GitHub that is not a real constraint.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />

  <!--
    The launcher icon is the blackletter S, on white — the mark, not the tool logo. The swiss army
    knife belongs inside the app where there is room to read it; at the size Android draws a home
    screen icon, twelve overlapping blades become a smudge.
  -->
  <!--
    android:name points at SaftApplication, whose only job is to install the crash handlers before
    any activity exists. It has to be named HERE: this element is written by hand, so it wins over
    the [Application] attribute in the C#, and without the name that class is simply never used -
    which was true for one build, silently.

    The crc64... prefix is not arbitrary and not ours to choose: .NET Android derives it from the
    namespace, so every type in SAFT.Droid lands in that same generated Java package. It is stable
    as long as the namespace is. If the namespace ever changes, this line changes with it.
  -->
  <application
    android:name="crc64662697f02d4601a8.SaftApplication"
    android:label="SAFT"
    android:icon="@drawable/saft_icon"
    android:requestLegacyExternalStorage="true" />
</manifest>
